Bank analysis links spreads, credit losses, costs, liquidity, and regulatory equity capital.|Loan growth raises income but also capital and loss exposure.|Industrial EBITDA alone adequately values a bank?|No|Yes|Only for listed banks
NBFC and housing lenders require loan-quality, funding, ALM, capital, and collateral analysis.|Long mortgages funded by short wholesale borrowing create refinancing exposure.|Collateral removes funding mismatch risk?|No|Yes|Only for housing
Insurer analysis combines underwriting, reserves, investment returns, capital, and liability matching.|Underwriting losses are temporarily offset by investment gains.|Overall profit proves underwriting strength?|No|Yes|Only in life insurance
Asset managers earn fees on relevant assets and flows; brokers depend on activity and fee economics.|Assets ₹1,000 at 1% fee produce ₹10 gross revenue before other terms.|Higher AUM always implies identical fee revenue growth?|No|Yes|Only for mutual funds
Exchange businesses earn from trading, listings, data, and services under regulatory and resilience constraints.|Higher trading volume raises some fees but increases operating demands.|Assess only transaction revenue?|No|Yes|Only for derivatives exchanges
SaaS uses recurring revenue and retention; IT services often use billable capacity, rates, and utilisation.|100 staff × 80% utilisation × ₹10 billing gives ₹800 capacity-based revenue.|100% headcount growth guarantees 100% revenue growth?|No|Yes|Only offshore
Marketplace economics separate GMV, take rate, incentives, fulfilment, and returns.|GMV ₹1,000 at 10% take rate yields ₹100 revenue before costs.|GMV equals revenue?|No|Yes|Only for e-commerce
Consumer and retail analysis tracks volume, mix, pricing, inventory, stores, and distribution.|Sales growth driven by discounts can weaken contribution.|Growth alone proves stronger unit economics?|No|Yes|Only for premium brands
Auto and EV finance reflects production scale, cycles, tooling, warranties, batteries, and funding.|New capacity requires cash before vehicle sales begin.|Order reservations automatically equal recognised revenue?|No|Yes|Only for EVs
Manufacturing and capital goods depend on orders, capacity, input costs, execution, and working capital.|Long lead times create inventory and customer-advance effects.|Backlog guarantees profitable cash collection?|No|Yes|Only for machinery
Mining and metals depend on commodity prices, reserves, costs, sustaining capex, and cycle exposure.|Peak metal prices inflate current margins.|Peak margins should be extrapolated forever?|No|Yes|Only for low-cost mines
Oil, gas, and petrochemicals involve different upstream, refining, and chemical spreads and risks.|Higher crude price helps some producers while hurting some input users.|All energy firms benefit identically from higher crude?|No|Yes|Only vertically integrated firms
Power and utility models depend on tariffs, capacity, availability, fuel, collection, and regulation.|A renewable project depends on generation and buyer payments.|Installed capacity alone determines cash generation?|No|Yes|Only for solar
Telecom and digital infrastructure require capex, spectrum or network assets, usage, pricing, and retention.|Higher customers coincide with lower revenue per user.|Revenue growth must equal customer growth?|No|Yes|Only for mobile networks
Pharma and biotech involve patent, trial, approval, pricing, and R&D risks; healthcare adds service operations.|A failed trial sharply reduces expected product cash flows.|R&D spending guarantees a marketable product?|No|Yes|Only in late-stage trials
Real estate and construction link project cash, completion, collections, debt, and recognition rules.|Bookings precede cash collection and qualifying revenue recognition.|Bookings always equal current recognised revenue?|No|Yes|Only for apartments
Airlines, airports, ports, and logistics have different demand, capacity, pricing, and cost sensitivities.|Higher fuel prices can hurt airline margins despite strong passenger demand.|Passenger growth alone proves earnings growth?|No|Yes|Only for low-cost airlines
Hospitality analysis uses occupancy, average rates, capacity, food costs, and operating leverage.|Hotel occupancy 80% × average daily rate ₹100 gives ₹80 revenue per available room.|RevPAR is…?|₹80|₹100|₹180
Agribusiness depends on yields, weather, commodity pricing, storage, seasonality, and biological-asset rules.|A good harvest raises volume but market prices fall.|Higher output guarantees higher revenue?|No|Yes|Only for farmers
Media and sports finance depends on rights, advertising, subscriptions, attendance, and talent costs.|A rights fee is paid before uncertain audience monetisation.|Audience size alone guarantees profitability?|No|Yes|Only for streaming
Education and professional services depend on enrolment or billable utilisation, pricing, retention, and staff capacity.|More professionals with low utilisation raise costs faster than fees.|Headcount growth guarantees profit growth?|No|Yes|Only for consulting
Government enterprises and regulated businesses combine commercial, policy, tariff, and ownership constraints.|A regulator limits prices despite increasing costs.|Ordinary free-pricing assumptions apply automatically?|No|Yes|Only if listed
